The Security Council,

Reaffirming its resolutions 1540 (2004), 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2314 (2016), 2319 (2016) and 2401 (2018),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and that any actions taken must strictly adhere to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Reiterating its condemnation in the strongest terms of the use of any toxic chemical as a weapon by anyone, anywhere, and under any circumstances in the Syrian Arab Republic, and underscoring that those responsible for any such use must be held accountable,

Expressing grave concern regarding recent allegations of the use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, in particular the reported incident in Douma on 7 April 2018,

Emphasizing the vital importance of ensuring that any investigation into allegations of the use of chemical weapons is conducted in a strictly professional, impartial, comprehensive, objective, and depoliticized manner, in full accordance with the Chemical Weapons Convention and established international standards,

Underlining the necessity of strictly preserving the chain of custody for all physical evidence and samples, as well as the indispensable requirement of on-site visits to incident locations to collect direct, verifiable, and untainted evidence,

Noting the official communication from the Syrian Arab Republic inviting the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) to dispatch a Fact-Finding Mission to visit Douma to investigate allegations of the use of chemical weapons, and welcoming the readiness of the Syrian authorities to provide all necessary facilitation and security guarantees for this purpose,

Recognizing the primary responsibility of the Security Council under the Charter of the United Nations for the maintenance of international peace and security,

1. Welcomes the decision of the Technical Secretariat of the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) to immediately deploy its Fact-Finding Mission (FFM) to the Syrian Arab Republic to establish the facts surrounding the allegations of the use of toxic chemicals as weapons in Douma on 7 April 2018;

2. Decides to establish, for an initial period of one year with the possibility of renewal, a United Nations Independent Mechanism of Investigation (UNIMI) to identify, to the greatest extent possible, individuals, entities, groups, or governments who were perpetrators, organizers, sponsors, or otherwise involved in the use of chemicals as weapons, including chlorine or any other toxic chemical, in the Syrian Arab Republic;

3. Stresses that UNIMI shall operate on the basis of principles of impartiality, independence, transparency, and high professional standards, and that its staff shall be recruited on a wide geographical basis in accordance with Article 101 of the Charter of the United Nations;

4. Directs that UNIMI and the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission shall base their conclusions on incontrovertible evidence collected directly on-site by their experts, with strict adherence to established verification procedures, rigorous chain-of-custody protocols for environmental and biomedical samples, and direct interviews with witnesses and victims, rather than unverified open-source materials or information provided by partisan entities;

5. Demands that all parties in the Syrian Arab Republic cooperate fully and immediately with the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission and UNIMI, granting their personnel safe, secure, unfettered, and unimpeded access to all relevant locations, individuals, and documentation necessary for their work;

6. Calls upon the Syrian authorities, as well as Member States with influence on the ground, to extend all necessary assistance, including security arrangements, to ensure the prompt, unhindered, and safe conduct of on-site investigations in Douma and any other relevant areas;

7. Requests the Secretary-General, in coordination with the Director-General of the OPCW, to submit to the Security Council within thirty days of the adoption of this resolution the terms of reference and practical modalities for the operation of UNIMI for the Council's review and approval;

8. Further requests the Director-General of the OPCW to report to the Security Council on the progress and findings of the Fact-Finding Mission regarding Douma as a matter of priority;

9. Underscores that the final assessment of the findings of UNIMI, as well as the determination of any measures to be taken against those identified as responsible for the use of chemical weapons, shall remain the prerogative of the Security Council, acting in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations;

10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

